00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.520
biggest misconception about YouTube.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.080
People think that the algorithm is

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.120
working against them. They think that

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.240
YouTube is hiding their videos on

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.800
purpose, or they think that they're

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:51.760
shadow banned. And that is just not how

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.600
it works in 99% of cases. The algorithm

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.320
has one job. It shows videos to people

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.840
who are most likely to watch them.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.920
That's literally it. When you upload a

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.640
video, YouTube pushes it to a small

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.240
group. If those people click on it and

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.080
watch it, the algorithm pushes it to

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.760
more people. And if they don't click on

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.000
it, or they click and leave right away,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.960
it stops pushing it. It is not personal.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.000
The algorithm is not out to get you.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.480
It's just doing math. So, when people

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.800
tell you to post at exactly 5:00 p.m. on

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:19.120
Tuesday, that's nonsense. The algorithm

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.080
does not care what time you post. All it

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.640
cares about is whether people click and

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.760
whether they keep watching. That is the

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.800
entire game right there. And I know this

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.680
because I used to post around 3:00 or

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.600
4:00 in the afternoon in the United

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.920
